    "case_id": "496",
    "case_summary": "A 52-year-old man with HCV-associated end-stage cirrhosis underwent liver transplantation. Post-surgery, he required relaparotomy the next day due to intra-abdominal bleeding from a choledochal artery branch. Initial liver function improved, but on day 4, cholestasis was observed with worsening cytolysis. On day 6, the patient reported persistent abdominal pain. By day 7, he developed fulminant liver failure and hemodynamic shock. On day 8, he experienced a single fever peak (38.4°C). The patient died on day 9 during laparotomy.\n\nPhysical examination findings were not explicitly detailed in the case report.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': ['Day 6 liver function tests:', '- Aspartate aminotransferase (AST): 5.04 μkat/L (normal 0.10–0.89 μkat/L)', '- Alanine aminotransferase (ALT): 5.14 μkat/L (normal 0.13–0.67 μkat/L)', '- γ-Glutamyltransferase (GGT): 6.61 μkat/L (normal 0.17–0.84 μkat/L)', '- Alkaline phosphatase (ALP): 6.47 μkat/L (normal 0.67–2.15 μkat/L)', '- Total bilirubin: 119 μmol/L (normal 0–21 μmol/L)'], 'Microbiological Tests': ['- Blood culture (day 7): Positive', '- Liver graft, colon, and small bowel biopsy samples (day 9): Positive', '- Hospital food cultures: Negative', \"- Donor's blood and peritoneal fluid cultures: Sterile\"], 'Surgical Findings': 'Emergency laparotomy revealed diffuse liver necrosis without vascular thrombosis or biliary leakage'}",
